1. What is an Exception?

An exception is an unexpected event that disrupts the normal flow of program execution.

Examples:
  Divide by zero
  Null pointer access
  File not found
  Database connection failure  

2. Why Exception Handling Is Important
  Prevents application crash
  Provides meaningful error messages
  Maintains application flow
  Mandatory for enterprise & production applications
  Critical in Spring Boot REST APIs

3. Exception Hierarchy
Throwable
 ├── Error
 │     └── OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError
 └── Exception
       ├── Checked Exceptions
       │      └── IOException, SQLException
       └── Unchecked Exceptions
              └── RuntimeException
                     └── NullPointerException, ArithmeticException

4. Types of Exceptions
1. Checked Exceptions
  Checked at compile time
  Must be handled or declared

Examples:
  IOException
  SQLException
  FileNotFoundException
  FileReader fr = new FileReader("file.txt"); // Compile-time error

2. Unchecked Exceptions
  Occur at runtime
  Subclasses of RuntimeException

Examples:
  NullPointerException
  ArithmeticException
  ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

3. Errors
  Serious system issues
  Not handled by application
Examples:
  OutOfMemoryError
  StackOverflowError

5. try-catch Block
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          try {
              int a = 10 / 0;
          } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
              System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
          }
      }
  }
  
  Output
  Cannot divide by zero

6. Multiple catch Blocks
  try {
      String s = null;
      System.out.println(s.length());
  } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
      System.out.println("Arithmetic exception");
  } catch (NullPointerException e) {
      System.out.println("Null pointer exception");
  }

Output
Null pointer exception
⚠️ Child exception must come before parent

7. finally Block
    Executes always (except JVM crash)
    Used for resource cleanup
    
    try {
        int a = 10 / 2;
        System.out.println(a);
    } finally {
        System.out.println("Finally block executed");
    }
    
    Output
    5
    Finally block executed

8. try-catch-finally
  try {
      int a = 10 / 0;
  } catch (Exception e) {
      System.out.println("Exception handled");
  } finally {
      System.out.println("Cleanup code");
  }
  Output
  Exception handled
  Cleanup code

9. try-with-resources (Java 7+)
  Automatically closes resources.
  try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"))) {
      System.out.println(br.readLine());
  } catch (Exception e) {
      System.out.println("Exception occurred");
  }
  ✔ Preferred over finally for closing resources
  ✔ Used heavily in enterprise applications

10. throw Keyword
  Used to explicitly throw an exception.
  public class Test {
      static void validate(int age) {
          if (age < 18)
              throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not eligible");
      }
  
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          validate(16);
      }
  }
  
  Output
  Exception in thread "main" java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Not eligible

11. throws Keyword
    Used to declare exception to caller.
    void readFile() throws IOException {
        FileReader fr = new FileReader("file.txt");
    }

12. Custom Exceptions
    Creating Custom Exception
    class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {
        InvalidAgeException(String msg) {
            super(msg);
        }
    }
    
    Using Custom Exception
    class Test {
        static void checkAge(int age) throws InvalidAgeException {
            if (age < 18)
                throw new InvalidAgeException("Age must be >= 18");
        }
    
        public static void main(String[] args) throws InvalidAgeException {
            checkAge(16);
        }
    }

13. Exception Propagation
    Unchecked exceptions propagate automatically.
    void m1() {
        int a = 10 / 0;
    }
    
    void m2() {
        m1();
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Test().m2();
    }
  ➡ Exception propagates until handled.

  
14. Difference: throw vs throws
| Feature | throw                      | throws              |
| ------- | -------------------------- | ------------------- |
| Purpose | Explicitly throw exception | Declare exception   |
| Usage   | Inside method              | Method signature    |
| Count   | Single exception           | Multiple exceptions |

16. Spring Boot Exception Handling (VERY IMPORTANT)
  @ExceptionHandler
  @ExceptionHandler(NullPointerException.class)
  public ResponseEntity<String> handleNPE() {
      return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Null value error");
  }
  
  @ControllerAdvice (Global Exception Handling)
  @RestControllerAdvice
  public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
  
      @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
      public ResponseEntity<String> handleAll(Exception ex) {
          return ResponseEntity.internalServerError()
                  .body(ex.getMessage());
      }
  }

✔ Centralized handling
✔ Clean controllers
✔ Production-ready APIs

17. Checked vs Unchecked (Interview Favorite)
| Checked           | Unchecked                |
| ----------------- | ------------------------ |
| Compile-time      | Runtime                  |
| Must handle       | Optional                 |
| Extends Exception | Extends RuntimeException |

18. Tricky Interview Questions
Q1. Can finally block be skipped?
➡ Yes, if System.exit() is called or JVM crashes.

Q2. Can we have try without catch?
➡ Yes, with finally.

Q3. Can we throw multiple exceptions?
➡ Yes, using multi-catch.
catch (IOException | SQLException e)

Q4. Can constructor throw exception?
➡ Yes.

Q5. What happens if exception occurs in finally?
➡ Original exception gets suppressed.
